 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "argon behaves much the same at 300 K as ethane does at 600 K",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "reduced-variables",
   "prompt": "<p>Argon and ethane have critical temperatures of 150.72 K and 305.4 K. At what temperature is ethane in a state corresponding to argon at 225 K, the reduced pressures being equal?</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "value": 456,
    "unit": "K",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Corresponding states share the same reduced temperature.",
    "solution": "<p>Argon at 225 K has $T_r = 225\\ \\text{K} / 150.72\\ \\text{K} = 1.493$. Ethane is in the corresponding state at the same $T_r$, so $T = 1.493 \\times 305.4\\ \\text{K} = 456\\ \\text{K}$.</p>"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "source_id": "The compressibility value ($Z$) of 3/8 at the critical point for the van der Waals equation is actually not in such good agreement with measurement.",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Analyze",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "reduced-variables",
   "prompt": "<p>Use the van der Waals critical constants $V_c = 3b$, $P_c = a/27b^2$ and $T_c = 8a/27bR$ to find the compressibility factor $Z = P_cV_c/RT_c$ at the critical point. How does it compare with the lowest point of the $T_r = 1.0$ curve in Figure 16.4.1?</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "value": 0.375,
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Substitute the three constants; a and b cancel.",
    "solution": "<p>$Z_c = \\dfrac{(a/27b^2)(3b)}{R\\,(8a/27bR)} = \\dfrac{a/9b}{8a/27b} = \\dfrac{3}{8} = 0.375$, the same for every van der Waals gas. The measured $T_r = 1.0$ curve falls to about 0.2 at $P_r = 1$, so the van der Waals equation puts $Z$ at the critical point well above what real gases show.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
